WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.140
. It's great to have back on the program after
I don't know how many years really, it feels

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.750
like a long time ago.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:12.379
Holyoke, Massachusetts Mayor Alex Morse, who
now also is candidate for Congress in Massachusetts,

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:14.650
1st Congressional District, my old district.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.449
I don't live there anymore.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.830
But it was it was my district for a while.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:19.830
Alex, it's great to have you on.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:20.830
Yeah.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:21.830
Thanks so much for having me back, David.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.259
I think it's been about nine years.

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:24.609
I think it has been that long.

00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:25.609
Yeah.

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:26.910
At the time, we were both much younger last
time.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.910
Yeah.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:28.910
Never be the same.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:30.400
We look exactly the same.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:31.400
That's the good news.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.400
Yeah.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.610
At least for now, five more years will be
a little different.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.940
Anyway, if Trump wins for sure.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:42.180
OK, so you're mayor of Holyoke and you're
running this Democratic primary campaign.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.670
The primary is on September 1st.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.500
People should already have requested and hopefully
have in hand ballots if they're voting absentee.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.370
I know people that have already sent them
back in.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.190
So this this is all sort of happening right
now.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.320
Talk a little bit about your approach here.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.580
As someone who is up against the longtime
incumbent in Richard Neal, someone that is

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.860
seen by some as part of the establishment,
someone that is seen by others as someone

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:15.250
who maybe is doing a good enough job, aware
where they would not want to see him replaced.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:16.570
What's your approach here?

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:17.570
Yeah.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.880
So we launched this campaign about 13 months
ago.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.140
We knew it would take a great deal of work
to build the kind of movement across Western

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.140
Mass.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:30.330
This is a district of eighty seven cities
and towns from the hill towns to the Berkshires,

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.500
rural parts of this district, down to urban
communities like Springfield and Holyoke.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:40.630
And so, you know, we have a member of Congress
now that just is unable to grasp the urgency

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.210
of the moment on every issue.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.210
He's absent.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:44.210
He's unaccountable.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.320
And he's been there for over 30 years.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.740
And it's a different time than it was in the
late 1980s.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:55.970
And it requires a different a different sort
of lens of which we view policy and just different

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:58.570
solutions to the challenges that we now have.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.420
And so our goal has always been to build a
grassroots campaign.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.400
We always knew we would get outspent and outraised.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.849
And our goal was to make sure that we have
the people power, the grassroots campaign

00:02:08.849 --> 00:02:12.030
and the resources that we need to defeat this
congressman.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:16.570
On September 1st and you know, twelve months
later and 13 days out from the election, we

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.650
are well on our way to doing that.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:23.060
And it's a testament to the people that have
just felt forgotten about and left behind

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.379
by the federal government in recent years.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.379
So we'll talk.

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:31.370
You've had some big boosts in fundraising
over the last seven days, and we'll talk about

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.829
the genesis of all of that in a bit.

00:02:33.829 --> 00:02:38.490
But as you point out, this is a district that
includes Springfield, Massachusetts, a city

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:45.480
that has a very, very different circumstances
and problems than places like I think Huntington

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:51.689
is also part of the district or, you know,
another very rural small town with one stoplight,

00:02:51.689 --> 00:02:52.769
if anything.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.709
So, I mean, what what Huntington needs and
what Springfield needs are two very, very

00:02:57.709 --> 00:02:59.200
different things.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.819
How do you assess working on both?

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:02.819
Yeah.

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:08.489
And so just yesterday we announced the support
of over 30 rural elected officials and community

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:14.669
leaders who feel very much forgotten about
and left behind that the perception now is

00:03:14.669 --> 00:03:17.760
not only do we have a member of Congress that
hasn't had a town hall in the district in

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.129
over three years, but people want a member
of Congress that shows up, in particular in

00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:24.689
the Hill towns and in the rural parts of the
district.

00:03:24.689 --> 00:03:30.120
And whether you live in Holyoke or Springfield
or Huntington or Chester or North Adams, people

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.749
are feeling, again, not represented by this
current occupant of this seat.

00:03:35.749 --> 00:03:36.749
And you would never know.

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:39.400
We have one of the most powerful members of
Congress representing us.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.800
When you look at outcomes, the fact that dozens
of cities and towns still are without basic

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.819
access to broadband Internet, that we have
public schools closing in the hill, towns

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:50.629
that can't afford the costs of municipal health
insurance.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:53.559
We have hospitals closing one in North Adams.

00:03:53.559 --> 00:03:58.079
Hundreds have been patient psychiatric beds
closing here and Holyoke and in Camden County

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:01.810
and Springfield, still the asthma capital
of our entire country.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:05.230
And I've made a commitment to having a rural
desk, making sure that we have a staff and

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:10.199
a presence in the hill towns and in the rural
parts of this district to make sure that everybody

00:04:10.199 --> 00:04:14.489
that lives here in western and central mass
knows that they have an ally in Washington.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:19.500
So most of my audience obviously doesn't live
in the Massachusetts 1st Congressional District,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.500
but they live in the United States.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:27.949
And so if you were to become a member of Congress,
you would be one of 435 votes on a whole bunch

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:33.379
of things that would affect people who are
in Florida or Los Angeles or wherever.

00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:38.660
So I'd like to talk a little bit about your
approach to some of the big issues right now

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.879
that can sometimes be dividing lines when
we think about who should be representing

00:04:42.879 --> 00:04:43.879
us.

00:04:43.879 --> 00:04:46.400
So, like, for example, big picture on taxation.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:52.310
We had a tax reform bill in 2017 from the
current administration and Republicans.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.080
There's a view that taxes should be much.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.020
Higher on some people or lower on others or
whatever the case may be to pick an issue

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.870
that is relatable to everybody.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.540
What's your perspective on what Congress should
be doing on taxes?

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.540
Yeah, and I would.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.199
I'll get to taxes in a moment.

00:05:09.199 --> 00:05:13.479
But this is a race, and I want listeners to
know that has national implications because

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.240
I'm challenging the chairman of the House
Ways and Means Committee.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.949
And I'm not contesting the fact that Congressman
Neal has power.

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:21.840
He has power as the chair of the Ways and
Means Committee.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.340
But he's not using his power.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.340
No.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.439
One to hold this president accountable.

00:05:25.439 --> 00:05:30.030
He dragged its feet for months to even request
the president's tax returns, refused to seize

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.599
the New York state returns that lawmakers
there worked for years to make available.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:39.069
He was one of the last congressional Democrats
to embrace and support an impeachment agree

00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:40.389
into this president.

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:44.310
And along with Elliot Engle is the only other
Democratic chair and leadership that hasn't

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:49.810
had a single oversight hearing on any component
of the Trump administration since the Democrats

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:50.810
took back the House.

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:57.080
And so instead of using his power for oversight,
he's using his power to again be complicit.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:01.689
And many of the priorities of the Trump administration
is an ardent opponent of Medicare for all.

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:05.689
Even as millions of Americans are losing their
job and their employer based health insurance

00:06:05.689 --> 00:06:10.449
is the only member of Congress here in Massachusetts
that refuses to sign on to the Green New Deal.

00:06:10.449 --> 00:06:15.060
He, even in the middle of this pandemic, has
come out against a monthly recurring payment

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.139
of two thousand dollars to the most vulnerable
Americans that are being left behind.

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:23.870
He has opposed Pramila Jiah Paul's Paycheck
Guarantee Act, and right now he is literally

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.810
the number one recipient of corporate money
in the entire House of Representatives, more

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:28.939
than any Democrat.

00:06:28.939 --> 00:06:31.310
And even more than every Republican.

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:36.189
And in regards to taxation in particular,
yes, this is a Congress, a Democrat that is

00:06:36.189 --> 00:06:38.650
openly opposed to a wealth tax.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.080
He has supported the carried interest loophole.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:46.129
I mean, the Ways and Means Committee in particular
is the committee that should be doing oversight

00:06:46.129 --> 00:06:49.719
hearings on the 2017 tax package.

00:06:49.719 --> 00:06:51.509
He has done nothing to address it.

00:06:51.509 --> 00:06:53.419
He has done nothing to raise the corporate
tax rate.

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:58.289
Instead, he is using his power to mitigate
the possibility politically of achieving a

00:06:58.289 --> 00:06:59.289
wealth tax.

00:06:59.289 --> 00:07:03.599
And if we defeat Congressman Neal on September
1st, it's all but assured that there will

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:07.090
be a member of the Progressive Caucus that
becomes chair of the Ways and Means Committee

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.639
that supports Medicare for all that supports
the Green New Deal, that supports a wealth

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:14.800
tax and a higher corporate tax rate that we
can actually invest in domestic priorities.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.120
That would make a difference in people's lives
here in western Mass.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.120
OK.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:22.430
But to get back to my question a little bit
on, let's go.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.430
Even more detailed then.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.749
So you mentioned a wealth tax.

00:07:25.749 --> 00:07:28.590
You mentioned a recurring payment during during
the pandemic.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:32.949
Our current top marginal tax rate right now
is in the mid 30s.

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:35.810
Where do you think the top marginal tax rate
should be?

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:42.479
Do you want to see a higher bracket at 45,
50, 70 percent as some other potential future

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:44.080
colleagues of yours have suggested?

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.259
Where would you put that?

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:50.000
Yeah, I'm much more in line with Elizabeth
Warren's concept of a wealth tax that the

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.550
wealthiest one percent.

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.479
Be it a penny or two on every dollar over
a certain amount of money.

00:07:56.479 --> 00:08:01.990
And it is criminal in this country that working
families and middle class families are paying

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:08.139
essentially more in taxes to the federal government
than, you know, folks at Facebook and Google

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:09.160
and Amazon.

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:13.449
I mean, as a percentage of income, you mean
as a percentage of income?

00:08:13.449 --> 00:08:14.449
Absolutely.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:17.729
And I think we also need to take a closer
look at modern monetary theory.

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:21.389
Made these archaic views of the deficit and
government spending.

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:25.719
I mean, we have the resources we need to fight
climate change to provide health care as a

00:08:25.719 --> 00:08:28.560
fundamental human right to provide free public
college.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.750
But when you have Democrats like Congressman
Neal pedaling right wing talking points about

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:37.100
the deficit and our inability to make these
investments at the very time 60 percent of

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:42.590
our federal budget goes to the Pentagon, I
mean, we need members of Congress, not just

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:46.910
not just any Democrat, but Democrats that
actually understand this to go to Washington

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:51.160
and just change the conversation in regard
to these issues on Pentagon funding.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.930
How specifically would you propose reducing
it?

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:57.450
I mean, would you close bases in places like
Germany, for example?

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:02.850
Yeah, I think I think first and foremost,
we need to evaluate the necessity of having

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:05.470
dozens of military bases around the world.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.900
And I think we need to have a foreign policy
that isn't.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:14.900
Isn't eager to sort of get itself involved
in unnecessary coups if democratically elected

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:17.650
leaders around the world.

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:18.700
And let me think again.

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:21.290
60 percent of our federal budget goes to war
in defense.

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:23.070
I think it's completely unacceptable.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.780
And I think there was some good effort among
the Democrats to reduce it by 10 percent.

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.850
But I think I mean, that's the flaw.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.550
And I think we need to continue going further
than that.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:37.000
So this race, the Massachusetts 1st Congressional
District, has been not only does it have national

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.970
implications, as you point out, but it's also
become very interesting to all sorts of different

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.770
folks around the country over the last 10
days because of a controversy that started

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:54.220
when the Massachusetts College Democrats put
out a letter sort of generally alleging, but

00:09:54.220 --> 00:10:00.910
without naming any individuals or putting
names to it, alleged inappropriate actions

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:06.630
by you when it comes to personal relationships,
when you were teaching a course at the University

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:11.980
of Massachusetts and in your role there working
with with college Democrats at UMass.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:16.920
And this has exploded to the point where,
you know, Glenn Greenwald has been tweeting

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.210
about it.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.310
Ryan Green, all sorts of people I follow on
fit.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:25.190
You know, I've known you for a decade and
people that had I had never mentioned you

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.560
before or tweeting about it, this has exploded.

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:31.290
Now, you've been able to raise a lot of money
as a result.

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:35.040
And I want to talk about whether you think
maybe it has backfired to some degree.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:42.450
But first of all, the sort of core of it is,
do you accept that you have acted inappropriately

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:47.200
at any point in any way, or do you reject
the allegations on principle?

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.870
I reject the allegations on principle.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:56.360
I mean, evidence has now come to light that
essentially proves that this was a coordinated

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.680
political attack.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.620
On behalf of supporters of the congressmen
that were, in fact trying to curry favor with

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:06.920
one of the most powerful incumbents in Washington,
I mean, this was a story that was shopped

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.180
around to mainstream media for months.

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:13.140
And every journalist rejected it because it
was vague.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:14.140
It was nonspecific.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:15.330
No one would go on record.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:20.070
I mean, this was essentially a blog post that
was printed in a student newspaper that was

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:25.760
unamplified to the local media buys by media
here across Massachusetts and by national

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.070
media outlets.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:32.240
And now, thanks to the investigative reporting
of The Intercept and the journalist there,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.470
we have now I mean, pretty much by the day.

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.940
I've learned more and more behind the political
motivations of this that goes to the height

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.930
of the Massachusetts Democratic Party itself.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:47.700
And I have been very clear and unapologetic
about my identity as a young, single gay person

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:52.570
and not having to apologize for having relationships,
consensual relationships with other adult

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:55.310
men in my life.

00:11:55.310 --> 00:12:00.500
And do you say that there are individuals
associated with and looking to curry favor

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:01.540
with your opponent?

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:05.620
But at this point, do you allege that your
opponent either knew that this was being done

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.790
or was involved in any way?

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:13.860
I mean, I think it is certainly suspicious
and I will let the people of the district

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.800
come to their own conclusions, but I think
people are certainly seeing this for what

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.330
it is that three weeks before the most competitive
election that this member of Congress has

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:23.000
ever seen, this suddenly happens.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.960
I mean, it is no accident.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:26.620
It is no coincidence.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:28.500
And so people are seeing through it.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:32.320
And then the language that has been used in
response, I mean, age-Old, homophobic tropes.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.540
Yes, there have been youth, right.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:38.240
Gay men who are all too familiar with the
overpolicing of our personal lives and our

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:39.240
sex lives.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.892
I mean, this the response to this hasn't just
been an attack on me as a candidate and as

00:12:42.892 --> 00:12:44.150
a person.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:45.190
And it has had a great impact.

00:12:45.190 --> 00:12:49.120
But it's been an attack on all of us and our
entire community and certainly invigorated

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.670
our supporters, invigorated the people here
in the district, and has invigorated and inspired

00:12:53.670 --> 00:13:07.950
people to get off the sidelines into this
race that

00:13:07.950 --> 00:14:24.400
that we're

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:54.810
in involved before.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:15:32.110
Let's pause our conversation there with Holyoke,
Massachusetts, Mayor Alex Morse, who is, of

00:15:32.110 --> 00:17:11.110
course, a candidate

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:29.550
for Congress 
in the Massachusetts 1st Congressional District

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:36.780
Democratic primary.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:18:46.920
The full conversation with him will be up
on

00:18:46.920 --> 00:19:27.360
our YouTube channel, YouTube dot com slash

